{
 "idea": {
  "id": "short_pressure",
  "family": "short-flow",
  "title": "Rank stocks by how much of the day's off-exchange volume was sold short",
  "why": "Two stories pull opposite ways, and the test is which one the data backs. (1) Short sellers are informed: days of heavy shorting come before weak returns over the next week (Boehmer, Jones, Zhang 2008; Diether, Lee, Werner 2009). (2) In FINRA's off-exchange data most 'short' volume is a market maker selling short to fill a buyer, so a high share is really heavy buying, often retail, and the stock tends to follow it up (the reasoning behind SqueezeMetrics' dark-pool index). Each stock is measured against its own last 60 sessions, so names that are always heavily shorted don't dominate.",
  "source": "Boehmer, Jones, Zhang 2008; Diether, Lee, Werner 2009; SqueezeMetrics 2018 (DIX white paper)",
  "market": "us",
  "universe": {
   "min_price": 5,
   "min_adv": 20000000.0,
   "top": 1000
  },
